<div class="learn-box">
**What you will learn**

Separate an ATC-class definition from an ingredient study, define new users,
and measure covered days, persistence and discontinuation.
</div>

**You need:** [the installed packages and practice resources](book-00-start.html#before-you-start),
[concept discovery](book-04-vocabulary.html), and the preceding tutorial chapters.
Run these sections in order in a fresh `book-work` session. All observations are
public synthetic teaching data; the numerical results are not clinical evidence.

## Research question and vocabulary feasibility

For a prespecified ATC class, what fraction of the first treatment year is covered,
and when does the first treatment era end? First establish that the vocabulary
supports that class. The small GiBleed vocabulary has no ATC entries; an ATC-class
estimate is therefore unavailable on this practice resource. The executable
worked analysis below is explicitly a **celecoxib ingredient** study instead.
It teaches the mechanics without relabelling an ingredient as an ATC class.

```{r chapter-setup}
library(dsOMOPClient)
source("tools/book/display.R")
source("tools/book/connection.R")
source("tools/book/practice.R")
source("tools/book/case-helpers.R")
practice <- book_connect("gibleed")
conns <- practice$conns
atc <- ds.omop.concept.search(NULL, vocabulary = "ATC", standard_only = FALSE, conns = conns)$pooled
print(data.frame(ATC_classes_available = NROW(atc)))
```

On an ATC-enabled resource, search the intended class with `standard_only = FALSE`,
inspect its class level and descendants, check prevalence, then save that set.
Do not infer an ATC hierarchy from a drug's spelling.

## Find the executable ingredient set

```{r utilisation-discovery}
hits <- ds.omop.concept.search("celecoxib", domain = "Drug", vocabulary = "RxNorm", conns = conns)$pooled
knitr::kable(hits[c("concept_name", "standard_concept", "concept_class_id")])
ingredient_id <- hits$concept_id[trimws(hits$concept_name) == "celecoxib"]
knitr::kable(ds.omop.concept.descendants(ingredient_id, conns = conns)$pooled["concept_name"])
knitr::kable(ds.omop.concept.prevalence("drug_exposure", top_n = 10, scope = "pooled", conns = conns)$pooled)
mapping <- ds.omop.concept.relationships(ingredient_id, relationship_id = "Maps to",
  conns = conns, symbol = "omop")$pooled
children <- ds.omop.concept.descendants(ingredient_id, include_self = FALSE,
  conns = conns, symbol = "omop")$pooled
print(data.frame(Maps_to_rows = NROW(mapping), Descendant_rows = NROW(children)))
drug_set <- omop_concept_set(ingredient_id, descendants = TRUE)
```

## Define new users and the denominator

Require a 365-day washout and a complete next year of observation. This complete-
follow-up restriction simplifies the demonstration but can select healthier or
more stable patients. A real protocol may instead use each person's observed time.

```{r utilisation-population}
users <- omop_new_users(drug_set, washout_days = 365)
users$filters <- c(users$filters, list(omop_observed(after = 364)))
drug <- omop_event("drug_exposure", drug_set)
era <- omop_era(drug, gap = 30, by = "ingredient", stockpile = TRUE)
covered <- omop_variable(event = era, reducer = omop_reduce("covered_days",
  window = omop_window(0, 364), coverage = "effective_supply"))
persistence <- omop_variable(event = era, reducer = omop_reduce("first_era_days",
  window = omop_window(0, 364)))
utilisation <- omop_study(users, outputs = "baseline", baseline = list(
  covered = covered, persistence = persistence,
  pdc = omop_number_derive(list(covered = covered),
    book_expr(book_op("/", book_col("covered"), book_lit(365))), unit = "1"),
  discontinued = omop_number_flag(persistence, "lt", 365),
  fills = omop_variable(event = drug, reducer = omop_reduce("count", window = omop_window(0, 364)))))
book_symbols(recipe_execute(utilisation, out = c(baseline = "D_utilisation"), conns = conns))
knitr::kable(book_summary(dsBaseClient::ds.summary("D_utilisation$covered", datasources = conns)))
```

The stockpiled era carries overlapping supply forward under the declared rule.
Covered days count the union of effective supply days, avoiding double counting
overlap. Era persistence tolerates a declared 30-day gap; it is not the same as
covered days. Recorded prescribing is not proof of actual ingestion.

## PDC and discontinuation

Proportion of days covered (PDC) divides covered days by the 365 eligible days.
The native expression declares a dimensionless result (`unit = "1"`), rather
than retaining the covered-days coordinate after a display rescaling.
A first era shorter than 365 days is a protocol-defined discontinuation indicator
under this gap rule, not proof of permanent treatment cessation.

```{r utilisation-pdc}
book_symbols(recipe_execute(utilisation, out = c(baseline = "D_utilisation_analysis"), conns = conns))
knitr::kable(book_summary(dsBaseClient::ds.summary("D_utilisation_analysis$pdc", datasources = conns)))
```

Inspect the definition before interpreting these values. Changing stockpiling,
gap tolerance or the follow-up denominator changes the question even when the
column name remains PDC. Privacy checks still apply to the final table and releases.

## Exercises

**1.** Summarise persistence and compare its units with PDC.

<details><summary>Solution 1</summary>

```{r utilisation-persistence}
knitr::kable(book_summary(dsBaseClient::ds.summary("D_utilisation_analysis$persistence", datasources = conns)))
```

Persistence is measured in days; PDC is a fraction. A tolerated gap contributes
to an era's span but need not contribute covered supply days.

</details>

**2.** Count the discontinuation indicator.

<details><summary>Solution 2</summary>

```{r utilisation-discontinuation}
knitr::kable(book_counts(dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_utilisation_analysis$discontinued", datasources = conns)))
```

The indicator is specific to the first era and this complete follow-up cohort.
Subsequent restarting remains possible.

</details>

**3.** Save the ingredient definition. Would changing its name to an ATC code
turn this into an ATC-class analysis?

<details><summary>Solution 3</summary>

```{r utilisation-save}
set_file <- tempfile(fileext = ".rds")
saveRDS(drug_set, set_file)
identical(readRDS(set_file), drug_set)
```

No. Class membership comes from the vocabulary hierarchy, not the local label.

</details>

## Recap

Check vocabulary feasibility first. Ingredient utilisation is executable here;
ATC-class utilisation requires the missing classification vocabulary. Distinguish
covered supply, era persistence, PDC and protocol-defined discontinuation, and
state the denominator and follow-up selection explicitly.

**Next:** [23. Case study: a harmonised Table 1](book-23-case-table1.html).
